<div class="source-doc-poem">
  <p>
    MINERAL COUNTY HIGH SCHOOL CIRCA 1930's<br><br>
    I'm called "Mister" Mashburn, the stern man said<br>
    as every student nodded his or her head<br><br>
    you're here to learn, not to play around<br>
    and you'd better believe I'm as mean as I sound<br><br>
    your lady teacher is called Miss Bayne<br>
    all eyes turned to Tommy and Raymond E. Lane<br><br>
    the first semester would find this pair<br>
    getting special tutoring that would curl their hair<br><br>
    you won't talk, snore or sniffle while you're in my class<br>
    and I will have to expel any boy passing gas<br><br>
    we'll start off the day with Algebra One<br>
    then to Latin and Physics while you're still having fun<br><br>
    Miss Bayne has English in the little North room<br>
    better put on your coats, it's as cold as a tomb<br><br>
    there's a four holer toilet and a tin covered shed<br>
    where you'll find wood and coal to keep the heaters fed<br><br>
    if there's anything else....yes, what is it Joe?<br>
    Mom gave us castor oil and I really have to go<br><br>
    then Mildred screamed, leaped straight in the air<br>
    after Frankie whispered "there's a spider in your hair"<br><br>
    Bobby shook the grates on the old round heater<br>
    threw some bullets on the coals, prayed "forgive me St. Peter"<br><br>
    explosions rattled windows, soot and ash were flying<br>
    the boys were all laughing and the girls were all a'crying<br><br>
    Mister Mashburn doffed his sweater, threw his tie towards his chair<br>
    trotted down the aisle and grabbed Bobby by the hair<br><br>
    with his left arm holding Bobby and his right 'round Frankie's neck<br>
    he rammed their heads together and dropped them on the deck<br><br>
    thus, order was restored in Mister Mashburn's class<br>
    until Arlene hollered "Chuck, I think Cloy's passing gas"<br><br>
    my name is "Mister" Mashburn, I told you at the start<br>
    and until you call me "Mister" he can <del>fart and fart and fart</del> blow his desk apart (amended)<br><br>
    j jackson 1991
  </p>
</div>

<p class="source-doc-note"><em>The last line is shown as it stands on the sheet. The typed ending, "fart and fart and fart," is struck through, and "blow his desk apart (amended)" is written in by hand beside it.</em></p>

<p class="source-doc-note"><em>The same prank is told in prose in John's book "Echoes from the Thundermug," in <a href="/writings/echoes-from-the-thundermug/married-life-and-mining-camps-part-one/#college-recruits">"College Recruits,"</a> where Bob Blackburn puts a handful of .45-caliber cartridges in the high school's wood-coal stove. The two versions differ, and both are kept as John wrote them: in "College Recruits" it is superintendent Charles Mashburn who adds coal and shakes the ashes down, the girls run for Miss Krausnick's room, and the class is dismissed for the day; in the poem Bobby shakes the grates himself, the other teacher is Miss Bayne, and Mashburn restores order by knocking Bobby's and Frankie's heads together.</em></p>

<p class="source-doc-note"><em>"Chuck" is Charles Church, identified from John's high school yearbook. The other classmates are named only by first name, apart from Raymond E. Lane. Possible identifications are recorded on the <a href="/index/">Name Index</a> and are not confirmed. Miss Bayne has not yet been found in any other record.</em></p>

